  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nCooler temperatures are in store for the next several days following \nthe passage of a cold front this morning. With flow shifting out of \nthe northwest, additional wildfire smoke from the Pacific Northwest, \nBritish Columbia, and the Northwest Territories will be drawn in via \nthe jetstream. As clouds continue to dissipate over the course of \nthe afternoon, the sky may take on a hazy appearance as smoke is \ncarried southeastward. Air quality reductions will also be possible \nas some of the smoke mixes down to the surface with an Adi of around \n50 (yellow) late this afternoon and overnight into tomorrow.\n\nSeveral shortwaves are expected to propel through the region in the \ncoming days, first during the late morning/early afternoon hours on \nWednesday. Mid-level moisture improves poleside of the ridge \nlate morning, just in time for the arrival of the vortmax. \nOverall, though, northwestern Missouri still looks to be on the \n\"drier\" side of the moisture gradient, especially in the lower \nlevels. Despite a boost in mid-level moisture, there's not much \nconfidence in rain being able to overcome the much drier near- \nsurface layer. For now, only about a 10-20% chance of rain was \nincluded in the forecast for tomorrow afternoon in far northwest\nMissouri. \n\nThe next rainfall opportunity arrives late Wednesday evening into \nearly Thursday morning. Southwesterly flow along the upwind portion \nof the ridge coupled with the low-level jet setting up over western \nand central Kansas will allow tropical moisture to surge into the \nGreat Plains. While the best moisture returns are expected over \nsouthern Nebraska, PWATs locally are hovering around 1.5-1.75\" which \nis roughly within the 75th to 90th percentile of climatology. Even \nso, the heaviest rainfall will likely occur west and northwest of \nour area of concern. Portions of far eastern Kansas and western \nMissouri may be able to get about a tenth of an inch or so out of \nthis initial round, but the setup strongly favors considerable \nrainfall elsewhere. That said, though it doesn't look particularly \nconcerning or impactful at this time, there's about a 40-50% chance \nof seeing some rain during this period. \n\nThe more impactful and widespread round of rain and storms looks to \narrive Thursday night and continue into much of Friday. The setup is \nnot too dissimilar from that of the previous evening, however, the \npositioning of these features looks to be much more conducive for a \nheavy rainfall event. This is especially true with the low-level jet \naxis which looks to orient itself in a manner such that tropical \nmoisture is injected directly into eastern Kansas and \nwestern/central Missouri. PWATs are forecast between 2\" and 2.25\", \ncomfortably exceeding the 90th percentile of climatology with mid-\n/low-level RH cresting around 95% Friday morning. Preliminary \nassessment of forecast soundings late Friday morning/early Friday \nafternoon also depict a deep warm cloud layer which raises \nconfidence in efficient rainfall, though this may be limited \nsomewhat by a general lack of instability. Storm motion and training \npotential are other factors that will need to be closely monitored \nin the coming days. Currently, it appears that storms will be able \nto propagate just fast enough to remain progressive...though this \nmight not alleviate much of the concern for heavy rainfall/flooding \ngiven the confidence surrounding efficient warm rain processes. WPC \nhas retained the Slight Risk (Level 2 of 4) for excessive rainfall \nthat could lead to flash flooding associated with storms Thursday \nnight into Friday. Maximum PoPs currently sit around 60-80%, peaking \nin the overnight and early morning hours.",
